{#
    PurchaseOrder's own `actionBarTemplate` for `commercial_document_view.html.twig` — the buy-side
    twin of `admin/order/_tab_nav.html.twig`.

    A prior version of this file (`@Procurement/_document_action_bar.html.twig`) claimed PurchaseOrder
    "has only one GET route" and rendered flash messages in place of a tab bar. That was false: this
    document has the same three real GET routes Order's own tab bar is built from — detail (this
    page), edit, and a PDF — `purchase_order/edit.html.twig`'s own tab_nav block already links all
    three. Corrected here rather than left standing (#full-parity, 2026-09-15).

    Not built on `sales_document_action_bar.html.twig`: that shared partial also requires
    `cloneUrl`, and PurchaseOrder still has no Clone route — genuinely absent, not a difference this
    file invents an opinion about. A small, PO-specific bar with the same markup/class/id
    conventions is the honest fit.

    Lock/Unlock joined this bar with #759, in the same markup `sales_document_action_bar.html.twig`
    uses for the sell side — `document_is_locked()`/`document_lock()` now answer for a PurchaseOrder
    too, through `LockableDocumentCatalogue`.

    Expects: `order`. Edit is withheld where `PurchaseOrderController::edit()` would refuse the
    route anyway — `PurchaseOrder::canEditOnStatus()` (the status rule, `HasStatus`) carries the same
    reasoning `OrderController::isOrderLockedForEditing()` does for a frozen order — AND where a lock
    (the separate, deliberate-act rule) would refuse it too. The two never merge; both are asked.

    The "…" disclosure sits in the SAME left-aligned cluster as View/Edit/PDF, not pushed to the far
    right — `sales_document_action_bar.html.twig`'s own docblock says why: a bar with only two or
    three tabs used to leave a wide dead gap before a right-pushed menu. Print/Edit are left out of
    the menu itself: they are already the PDF/Edit tabs immediately to the left, and a document does
    not need the same action reachable twice in one bar.

    It is a real `<details>` overflow disclosure — `no-js-row-actions--overlay`, the same shell
    `sales_document_action_bar.html.twig` uses for its own "…" More menu — not a flat row of
    buttons. A `style="display: flex"` span here (an earlier version of this file) defeats that
    shell entirely and dumps every secondary action, plus the Lock reason text box, side by side in
    the bar itself: exactly the "row of buttons" #full-parity's own #3 rule (one row-action-menu,
    not flat controls) exists to rule out, on the one screen with the most secondary actions to
    hide.
#}
{% include 'admin/_row_actions_no_js.html.twig' %}
{% set poLocked = document_is_locked(order) %}
{% set poLockRow = poLocked ? document_lock(order) : null %}
<nav class="company-action-links order-tab-nav" aria-label="Purchase order detail actions">
    <a id="po-action-view" class="button is-current" href="{{ path('admin_bundle_procurement_purchase_order', {id: order.id}) }}">View</a>
    {% if order.canEditOnStatus() and not poLocked %}
        <a id="po-action-edit" class="button" href="{{ path('admin_bundle_procurement_purchase_order_edit', {id: order.id}) }}">Edit</a>
    {% endif %}
    <a id="po-action-pdf" class="button" href="{{ path('admin_bundle_procurement_purchase_order_print', {id: order.id}) }}">Purchase Order PDF</a>
    <details class="row-action-menu no-js-row-actions no-js-row-actions--overlay">
        <summary id="po-action-more" class="mini-action" aria-label="More purchase order actions">&hellip;</summary>
        <div class="row-action-dropdown company-action-dropdown">
            {% if order.statusEnum.acceptsReceipts %}
                <a href="{{ path('admin_bundle_procurement_receive', {po: order.id}) }}">Receive against this</a>
                <a href="{{ path('admin_bundle_procurement_receive_scan') }}">Scan against this</a>
            {% endif %}
            <a href="{{ path('admin_bundle_procurement_bill_new', {po: order.id}) }}">Enter a bill</a>
            {% if order.status == 'Draft' %}
                <form id="po-action-issue-form" method="post" action="{{ path('admin_bundle_procurement_purchase_order_issue', {id: order.id}) }}" style="display: contents;">
                    {{ csrf_field() }}
                    <button id="po-action-issue" type="submit">Issue to vendor</button>
                </form>
            {% else %}
                <form id="po-action-send-form" method="post" action="{{ path('admin_bundle_procurement_purchase_order_send', {id: order.id}) }}" style="display: contents;">
                    {{ csrf_field() }}
                    <button id="po-action-send" type="submit">Send to vendor</button>
                </form>
            {% endif %}
            {% if order.statusEnum.acceptsReceipts and order.quantityOutstanding > 0 %}
                {# Close it short only offers itself while there is something outstanding TO close
                   short on — a fully received order has nothing left to write off, which is a
                   different fact from merely "not final" (matching the condition the pre-existing
                   purchase_order_detail.html.twig used: `status in [Issued, Partially Received,
                   Received] and quantityOutstanding > 0`). #}
                <form id="po-action-close-form" method="post" action="{{ path('admin_bundle_procurement_purchase_order_close', {id: order.id}) }}" style="display: contents;">
                    {{ csrf_field() }}
                    <input type="hidden" name="reason" value="Closed short from the detail screen.">
                    <button id="po-action-close" type="submit">Close it short</button>
                </form>
            {% endif %}
            {% if not order.statusEnum.isFinal %}
                <form id="po-action-cancel-form" method="post" action="{{ path('admin_bundle_procurement_purchase_order_cancel', {id: order.id}) }}" style="display: contents;">
                    {{ csrf_field() }}
                    <input type="hidden" name="reason" value="Cancelled from the detail screen.">
                    <button id="po-action-cancel" class="danger" type="submit">Cancel</button>
                </form>
            {% endif %}
            {% if poLocked %}
                <span id="po-action-lock-state">Locked by {{ poLockRow.locked_by }}{% if poLockRow.reason %} — {{ poLockRow.reason }}{% endif %}</span>
                {% if is_granted('ROLE_SUPER_ADMIN') %}
                    <form id="po-action-unlock-form" method="post" action="{{ path('admin_bundle_procurement_purchase_order_unlock', {id: order.id}) }}" style="display: contents;">
                        {{ csrf_field() }}
                        <button id="po-action-unlock" type="submit">Unlock Purchase Order</button>
                    </form>
                {% else %}
                    <span id="po-action-unlock-denied">Only a super admin can unlock this purchase order.</span>
                {% endif %}
            {% else %}
                {# The reason field used to sit inline in the dropdown/bar. A real
                   <form method="post"> in a hidden `.modal-backdrop` — the same shell
                   `admin/company/detail.html.twig`'s note modal uses (app.css:12932) — is a plain
                   POST a no-JS browser or a test can still submit directly; only the JS-driven
                   open/close is new (app.js, `.js-lock-modal-open`/`-close`). #}
                <button id="po-action-lock" type="button" class="js-lock-modal-open">Lock Purchase Order</button>
            {% endif %}
        </div>
    </details>
</nav>
{% if not poLocked %}
    <div class="modal-backdrop lock-modal" hidden>
        <form class="modal-card" method="post" id="po-action-lock-form" action="{{ path('admin_bundle_procurement_purchase_order_lock', {id: order.id}) }}" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-labelledby="po-lock-modal-title">
            {{ csrf_field() }}
            <h2 id="po-lock-modal-title">Lock Purchase Order</h2>
            <label class="note-modal-field">
                Reason (optional)
                <input id="po-action-lock-reason" type="text" name="reason" maxlength="255" aria-label="Reason this purchase order is locked">
            </label>
            <div class="modal-actions">
                <button class="button outline js-lock-modal-close" type="button">Cancel</button>
                <button class="button primary" type="submit">Lock Purchase Order</button>
            </div>
        </form>
    </div>
{% endif %}
{% include '@Procurement/_purchase_flash_messages.html.twig' %}
